# ── Internal paths: not published ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The publish directory is the repository root — there is no netlify.toml and
# no build step, so by default EVERY tracked file is served with a 200. That
# put the strategy notes, the internal runbooks, the generator source and the
# raw Research Center datasets on public URLs, none of which is linked from a
# page, listed in a sitemap, or fetched at runtime.
#
# The trailing "!" is load-bearing. Netlify serves an existing static file in
# preference to a redirect rule, so a rule without the force flag would match
# nothing here — every path below is a real file on disk. See
# https://docs.netlify.com/routing/redirects/rewrites-proxies/#shadowing
#
# Deliberately still public: /LICENSE.md, /README.md, /DATA-RIGHTS.md,
# /THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, /robots.txt, /sitemap*.xml, every page route, and
# the /css/, /js/ and /images/ assets the pages need.
#
# Blocking a path here does not remove any published content: the Research
# Center pages carry their data inline, baked in at build time by the
# generators, not fetched from /data/ by the browser.

# Internal strategy, roadmap and workflow notes.
/site-strategy.md            /404.html  404!
/seo-content-plan.md         /404.html  404!
/seo-implementation.md       /404.html  404!
/link-preservation-audit.md  /404.html  404!

# Internal documentation: runbooks, verification backlogs, release notes,
# development plans and design reviews.
/docs/*                      /404.html  404!

# Generator, validator and test source.
/scripts/*                   /404.html  404!

# Pre-render source fragments. These are inputs to build-static-pages.cjs; the
# published output lives at /privacy/, /terms/, /blog/ and friends. Serving the
# source as well duplicated those pages on a second URL.
/content/*                   /404.html  404!

# Separate Node application source; its own deployment does not run from here.
/startups-app/*              /404.html  404!

# Research Center datasets. The compiled records are the asset DATA-RIGHTS.md
# reserves; they were downloadable whole, one request per collection.
/data/*                      /404.html  404!

